    "body": "## Problem\r\n\r\nThis PR addresses a number of small issues with low-risk fixes.\r\n1. There is unnecessary padding between sections wasting space, the exact amount depending on which sections are visible.\r\n2. 'fillchars' are used where spaces are more appropriate (and vice versa, but this remains unfixed).\r\n3. Information can jump around more than necessary because of the section order.\r\n4. Some parts can be simplified.\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nUse existing 'stl'-DSL features, see commit message for details.\r\n\r\n## Open issues\r\n\r\nThe problem that I didn't fix are the spaces that should be fillchars, which can be seen immediately with `nvim --clean +\"set fcs+=stl:.\"`\r\n```\r\n[No Name]  ...........0,0-1......... All\r\n```\r\nThe old C implementation separated all sections by fillchars. But unfortunately, there doesn't seem to be a good way of producing them, although there are some workarounds like `%1(%)`. In my (weakly held) opinion:\r\n- such workarounds are confusing and should be avoided in the default\r\n- the spaces between directly adjacent sections are not a big deal\r\n- it's more important that sections separated by `%=` never touch, which affects all users, than to solve the cosmetic issue for fillchar users (for which there are workarounds)\r\n\r\nMy preferred solution would be to use #41600. The sequence `%= ` (separator + space) that ensures a minimum separation of 1 cell would be replaced by `%1=`. A single fillchar could be added with `%1.1=`, which doesn't save any keystrokes compared to `%1(%)`, but it is semantically clear and not a workaround.",
    "closed_at": "2026-09-03T16:44:31Z",
